-MultiscreenWindowManagementPermissionContextTest.IsExtendedCrossOriginDeny
-MultiscreenWindowManagementPermissionContextTest.IsExtendedSameOriginAllow
-WindowManagementTest.OnCurrentScreenChangeEvent
-WindowManagementTest.OnScreensChangeEvent
-WindowManagementTest.ScreenDetailedOnChange
-WindowManagementTest.ScreenDetailedOnHdrHeadroomChange

# Chromium test: race between document.cookie setter (CookieJar mojo pipe, async
# with AsyncSetCookie) and cookieStore.get() (CookieStore API, separate mojo pipe).
# These use independent RestrictedCookieManager pipes with no ordering guarantee.
# Brave's ephemeral storage checks (FillEphemeralStorageParams,
# IsEphemeralCookieAccessible) add processing overhead that widens the race window.
# Stable upstream: 0.2% flake rate over 30 days per LUCI Analysis.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/55325
-All/CookieStoreSameSiteTest.CookieStoreUsesEffectiveSameSiteForUnspecifiedCookies/0
-All/CookieStoreSameSiteTest.CookieStoreUsesEffectiveSameSiteForUnspecifiedCookies/1

# Chromium test: intermittent RunLoop timeout in DevTools paused debugger focus
# mechanism. The test relies on an async chain (DebuggerPaused event → SourcesPanel
# module loading → bringToFront IPC) that sometimes doesn't complete before the
# test timeout. Already disabled upstream on Windows ASAN (crbug.com/337141755).
# 0.2% upstream flake rate over 30 days per LUCI Analysis. No Brave-specific code
# affects this path.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/53358
-DevToolsProcessPerSiteTest.PausedDebuggerFocus

# These tests fail on Linux because kPromptForDownload defaults to true,
# triggering the GTK file chooser dialog which causes g_malloc leaks
# detected by LSan on ASAN builds and timeouts.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/52572
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/52649
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/52569
-All/PDFPluginDisabledTest.DirectNavigationToPDF/*
-ChromeMojoProxyResolverFactoryBrowserTest.*
-PluginResponseInterceptorURLLoaderThrottleBrowserTest.DownloadPdf

# These fail because OsIntegrationTestOverride::GetShortcutPath and
# OsIntegrationTestOverride::IsRunOnOsLoginEnabled on Linux hard-codes "chrome-"
# into the shortcut name but our shortcut starts with "brave-".
-ShortcutSubManagerBrowserTest.CleanUpEphemeralProfileDeletesShortcuts

# Chromium test: fixture sets kGlicActorPageStabilityTimeout to 30s but doesn't
# set kActorObservationDelayTimeout (defaults to 10s). On slow CI, the 10s
# overall timeout fires while still in kWaitForPageStability, causing the state
# to skip kWaitForLoadCompletion and jump directly to kDone. 0.5% upstream flake
# rate over 30 days per LUCI Analysis. No Brave chromium_src overrides affect
# the ObservationDelayController code path.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/54159
-ObservationDelayControllerTest.UsePageStabilityForSameDocumentNavigation

# Brave's canvas farbling (PerturbPixels) modifies getImageData() results,
# breaking this test's canvas-based video playback detection. The test only
# compiles on Linux non-sanitizer builds (#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)).
# Upstream stable: 640x480 0.6%, 3840x2160 0.3% flake rate (30-day LUCI).
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/54521
-All/GetDisplayMediaHiDpiBrowserTest.Capture/640x480
-All/GetDisplayMediaHiDpiBrowserTest.Capture/3840x2160

# This Chromium test fails because the Glic WebUI creates its webview
# asynchronously via JavaScript, and Brave's environment doesn't fully support
# the Glic WebUI initialization flow. Stable upstream (0.3% flake rate).
-GuestUtilBrowserTest.OnGuestAdded_Glic

# Chromium test: ApplyDefaultSearchChangeForTesting(FROM_EXTENSION) on Linux
# leaves default_search_provider_ null (extension DSEs unsupported on Linux)
# while default_search_provider_source_ stays FROM_EXTENSION. Brave's
# DefaultSearchManager override causes MaybeShowDialog() to record the
# histogram twice with differing conditions (kControlledByPolicy and
# kExtensionControlled), failing the ExpectUniqueSample assertion.
# Already disabled on Windows in upstream source; upstream TODO crbug.com/429600559
# acknowledges the broken extension DSE test setup.
# Stable upstream: 0.3% flake rate over 30 days per LUCI Analysis.
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/54542
-SearchEngineChoiceDialogBrowserTest.DialogDoesNotShowWithExtensionEnabledThatOverridesDSE

# This test is flakey upstream due to https://issues.chromium.org/issues/511552103
-All/TextfieldDumpAccessibilityEventsTest.TextSelectionChanged/linux_ViewsAXEnabled

# Upstream Chromium test (chrome/browser/webapps/installable/
# installable_manager_browsertest.cc). It asserts on InstallableManager's
# private task queue via friend access, and that assertion is racy whenever a
# second, unrelated GetData() request is in flight.
#
# InstallableTask::IncrementStateAndWorkOnNextTask() calls
# manager_->OnTaskFinished() (which POSTs FinishAndStartNextTask) and only then
# RunCallback(); CallbackTester::OnDidFinishInstallableCheck in turn POSTs the
# run loop's quit closure. So the pop-the-queue task is always ordered ahead of
# the quit closure. Separately, every primary page carrying a manifest reaches
# ManifestUpdateManager::OnManifestSeenOnPrimaryPage, which unconditionally
# schedules a ManifestSilentUpdateCommand; that command calls
# WebAppDataRetriever::CheckInstallabilityAndRetrieveManifest, i.e. a second
# InstallableManager::GetData(). When that request is queued while the test's
# own task is still running, FinishAndStartNextTask pops the test's task and
# immediately Start()s the background one, which completes off already-fetched
# page data and posts its own FinishAndStartNextTask. That post is still
# pending when the quit closure runs, so task_queue_.HasCurrent() is true at
# the assertion. Whether the background request lands inside that window is
# pure timing, hence the intermittency.
#
# Not caused by Brave. Brave has no chromium_src override or patch under
# components/webapps/browser/installable/, none under
# chrome/browser/web_applications/ outside os_integration/mac/ (macOS app
# shims), and none on the test itself. The only Brave override anywhere near
# this path is chromium_src/components/webapps/browser/features.cc disabling
# kWebAppsEnableMLModelForPromotion, and it is inert here: on this path that
# flag is read only by MLInstallabilityPromoter, and the fixture sets
# test::g_disable_banner_triggering_for_testing, which makes
# MLInstallabilityPromoter::StartPipeline() bail out before it starts any of
# its tasks.
#
# script/check-upstream-flake.py reports "STABLE UPSTREAM" (0.8% over 60 days),
# i.e. below the 1% bar, so this entry rests on the mechanism above and on
# Brave being provably not at fault, not on the flake rate. The rate only
# decides which file the entry belongs in: over that window linux-rel and
# win-rel both sit at ~0.2%, the Linux ASAN/LSAN builders show no failures at
# all, and macOS shows 1 flake in 10922 verdicts. The per-OS Ubuntu-22.04
# figure of 1.13% is dominated by ChromeOS builders, which Brave does not ship.
#
# So not browser_tests-linux-asan.filter: the race needs no sanitizer to occur,
# and upstream's own Linux ASAN/LSAN builders are the clean ones, so filtering
# only ASAN would leave the non-sanitizer Linux builds exposed. And not the
# all-platform browser_tests.filter: macOS shows no evidence of the failure, so
# there is no reason to drop coverage there, and Windows is already covered in
# browser_tests-windows.filter.
#
# Fixing this requires reordering the callback and the queue pop in upstream
# test or production code. A previous attempt, brave/brave-core#34786, patched
# the upstream test to add content::RunAllTasksUntilIdle() before the
# assertion; it was closed unmerged with no review comments. Independent of why
# it was closed, patching upstream test files is disallowed (TUF-001), and
# draining the run loop is a timing adjustment rather than synchronization.
#
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/58061 (Linux)
# https://github.com/brave/brave-browser/issues/53717 (Windows, closed)
-InstallableManagerBrowserTest.CheckWebapp

